WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:02.360
A clock edge launches a change.

2
00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.620
It travels through logic
cells and along a routed wire.

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.400
The next register needs a stable
input just before it samples.

4
00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.020
That's the setup deadline.

5
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.020
This change settles early.

6
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.280
The time left is positive slack.

7
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:18.160
Send the same transition
down a longer wire.

8
00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:20.160
Now it misses the deadline.

9
00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:21.720
The slack is negative.

10
00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.180
Where did the time go?

11
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.170
TimingLens opens a routed Vivado capture

12
00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:28.780
and groups failing paths
that share a cause.

13
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.720
Here, one control signal drives 192 loads.

14
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:34.720
Follow the branch to this lane.

15
00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.620
The launch cell takes less
than half a nanosecond.

16
00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.540
The wire takes 2.4 ns.

17
00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.010
The destination register has a source tag

18
00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:45.540
so we can see exactly which
line of code uses that control.

19
00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.740
That slow wire suggests
a routing experiment.

20
00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.200
Try fanout optimization on
a copy of the placed design,

21
00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:53.440
then route it again.

22
00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.030
For a deep logic path,
try balancing a priority selector

23
00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.640
or spreading arithmetic across
stages already in the pipeline.

24
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.500
If lookup tables are scarce,

25
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.260
a supported multiplier can
move into dedicated DSP blocks.

26
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.790
TimingLens can try supported changes

27
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.680
in an isolated project copy.

28
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:14.380
Experiment Lab compares setup,
hold, and resource use,

29
00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.020
so a regression is
visible alongside a gain.

30
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:21.060
Now take a countdown
timer targeting 250 MHz.

31
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.180
That's four nanoseconds per cycle.

32
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.570
The timer subtracts an accepted request

33
00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.560
only when there's enough count left.

34
00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.920
Follow the worst path to its endpoint.

35
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.200
It's the clock enable.

36
00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:35.680
The decision to subtract
arrives 633 ps late.

37
00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:37.740
Here's the change in the RTL.

38
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.200
The subtraction stays on the same line.

39
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.800
It's the guard that changes.

40
00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.570
Originally, the full 32-bit count

41
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.220
is compared with an unsigned
4-bit request.

42
00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.440
That answer combines
with accept to drive the enable.

43
00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.300
The routed path crosses four carry blocks.

44
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.180
The rewrite asks two
questions in parallel.

45
00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:57.940
Is any upper bit set?

46
00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.880
And are the low four bits
at least the request?

47
00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.640
Either answer is enough
when accept is high.

48
00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.460
Take 20 remaining and a request for 12.

49
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.680
The low bits of 20 say four.

50
00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.240
Four is too small.

51
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.380
But the upper bit tells us
the count is at least 16.

52
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.680
Every four-bit request fits.

53
00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:18.980
On the very same clock
edge, 20 becomes eight.

54
00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.100
Both versions passed
the same countdown test.

55
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.450
The two builds use
the same 4 ns constraint

56
00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.220
and the same Default recipe.

57
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.250
Worst setup slack went from −0.633 ns

58
00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:35.040
to +0.407 ns.

59
00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:37.940
32 failing setup
endpoints dropped to zero.

60
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.970
The standalone timer now meets 250 MHz.
